I've had some very intense, and unpleasant, experience with Digital
UNIX, so I'm going to throw my 2 cents in on this particular comment..
My problems particularly centered around the capabilities, or lack
thereof, of the LSM implementation.  To say that LSM is the equivalent
of the AIX LVM implementation is pretty much like saying that an
18-wheeler and 1/2 ton pickup are both the same because they are
trucks and can haul stuff.  Technically correct, but moving a few tons
of junk is a lot more work with the latter.  To wit...

The AIX LVM does a tremendous job in "hiding" the underlying hard disk
topology, unless you really need to get at it.  LSM on the other hand,
still forces you to get down and dirty with disk partitions, and such,
making admin and configuration a LOT of work.  ( Even if using RAID
controlled disk arrays.  We were using a Storage Works cabinet with
200+ GB of disk )

( My experience is with DU 4.0B so the following comment may no longer
be valid, but... )

Additionally, as was pointed out in another post in this thread, LSM
is NOT an internalized attribute of Digitial UNIX.  You still have to
install into a disk partition then "encapsulate" the root, usr, and
swap partitions.  Shades of HP/UX.

This post isn't meant to bash Digital UNIX so much as to point out
that it has a long way to go in some areas.  All the problems AIX had
with point release upgrades, unstable system processes and such are
happening to DU.  The Alpha hardware was awesome, I loved it, but
Digital UNIX wasn't anything to write home about.  ( Point of honesty:
I first got into Unix with AIX and grew to love it, so yes, my
opinions are somewhat biased in that regard. )

One other point to consider before jumping on the Digital UNIX
bandwagon: support.  I have no idea how things are now, ( I left
Digital UNIX behind a year ago), but DEC support of DU was awful.
(Considering the poor reputation that Compaq has for customer support,
I can't imagine the buyout is helping things now either.)  Every time
I placed a support call I had to spend ten minutes convincing the Tech
I wasn't running on VMS and explaining that YES, Digital had sold us a
UNIX system.  Then, an hour later I'd get a call back with a VMS "fix"
anyway.

All of the above, is of course, my own opinion, based on my experience
while working with both AIX and Digital UNIX in an environment mixed
with HP/UX and DG/UX too!  From a SysAdmin point of view, I rate these
players:

1) AIX
2) DG/UX
3) HP/UX
4) Digital UNIX

Take it for what it's worth.

Regards,

Michael West



