Well...I called some vendors and got prices for machines, 
then I went out and found people with those machines and ran 
our codes  (written in Fortran 90) on them.  Our codes are well
characterized (heavy floating point operations, with little I/O) so
we can get a good idea of a machine's performance for our applications
by running a battery of codes we've written in the past.  YMMV.  
I don't know anything about how the TPC-C benchmarks compare.  I have to
admit that I haven't kept up well on IBM prices since we started
buying Alphas a couple years ago.  My latest price for a 533 MHz
21164A Alpha w/256mb ram, 4.5gb SCSI-II hd, video + DU license is
<$4,100.  Our code executes about 4.5 times faster on one of these
than the older model 590.  These Alphas are also somewhat faster 
than the latest processor they installed at the Cornell Theory Center
(135MHz Power2 Super Chip) although I don't have specific numbers since
we already burned up our allocation for this year and I can't run new
benchmarks.   I might not be up on the latest IBM prices, but I'd be 
really suprised if they sold *any* machine that ran AIX for $4,100.

Better yet, my friend received this from someone at IBM.  It's pretty
damn funny when you realize you could by the same computing power 
under $15K (at the time, now it's down to $8K) if you bought two
Alpha workstations.

