Pierre BERGDOLT wrote:
> 
> Hello,
> 
> I have several DEC alpha/255 to install with exactly the same
> configuration. In the configuration I have to install there is a lot of
> products (DU4.0D, Cmise, X25, X400, X500, Mailworks,....) so I plan to
> setup one of the machine to be a remote installation server. Did anyone
> experiment this? Any comment or remarks will be greatly appreciated.
> 

Setting up and administering RIS is discussed in detail in the 
book "Sharing Software on a Local Area Network" (Here's a pointer
to the online version for V4.0D:

http://www.UNIX.digital.com/faqs/publications/base_doc/DOCUMENTATION/V40D_HTML/APS3LETE/TITLE.HTM

Another area you should investigate is the installation cloning ability
described in the V4.0D Installation Guide, Appendix C.
Here's a pointer for that:

http://www.UNIX.digital.com/faqs/publications/base_doc/DOCUMENTATION/V40D_HTML/AQTLGBTE/TITLE.HTM

Installation cloning lets you replicate installation parameters (file
systems, disklabels, subsets, etc.) across "like" systems via the use of
an answer file.  That file can be located on a RIS server, floppy disk,
as well as several other places that you can read about.  The
installation process also provides "hooks", or call-out points that
allow you customize the installation process for your specific
purposes.  One thing that you might want to do is load all your
additional layered software to be served by RIS, and script the
automatic loading of that software from the RIS server via the
"postload" hook. If you choose to do this, note that you'll need to
unset the environment variable "ADVFLAG" that the installation process
uses internally. This tells 'setld' to distinguish between 
loading base O/S software and layered software. This should only be done
in the "postload" hook.

Note that you will need a "model" system installed first, from which to
clone subsequent systems from.  By using the cloning process, you
should be able to boot your clients from the RIS server, and walk away. 
When you come back, they'll be installed and ready to login.

	-Steve Grandinetti
