jeremyp@gsms01.alcatel.com.au (Peter Jeremy) writes:

>We are currently looking at using NFS to share a filesystem between two
>AlphaServers running OSF1 4.0.D.  A question has been raised regarding
>the reliability of DEC's NFS implementation.

By whom?  Someone trying to make Irix 5.3 work with us?  :-)

>In the particular implementation, a file will be written by a process
>on the NFS server and read bu a process on the NFS client.  The two
>machines will be on the same 100baseTX segment.

>Is anyone aware of any NFS-related problems that would either:
>1) reduce the reliability of the either the NFS client or server;

No.  100 Mbps will leave a lot of time for CPUs to run emacs, watch
dynamic GIFs with netscape, etc.  If you had 2 or 3 fast interfaces
then any compute bound jobs might not get much time.

 or
>2) result in the client process not seeing updates written on the
>   server, without an error being reported on either machine.

This is a bit of a problem for any NFS implementation, as NFS is
generally tuned for performance and cache consistancy suffers.  Most
NFSes, ours included, use "close-to-open" consistancy, where open()
will cause the client to check the file's attributes on the server.
If the file size or mtime (time of last modification) is different,
then the client will discard any cached data and read from the server.
Without it, reads from cache will return stale data, reads from the
server include attributes, so that will flush stale data. (NFS V3
only.)

Alternatively, you can synchronize things with file record locking, but
that disables the local cache completely and you'll quickly learn why
people put up with cache consistancy quirks.
--
