In article <363286EE.DD70EFCC@mediaone.net>, byte56@mediaone.net writes:

>1.  How do I access the floppy drive after I  /dev/dfo, then I use the
>command tar -x...I get nothing.

The special for the floppy device on most systems is /dev/rfd0c.  If
you want to write a tar archive to it use something like:

tar cf /dev/rfd0c file-list

For extracting:

tar xf /dev/rfd0c

>
>2.  If  I want to add an external driver on the scsi bus connection, do
>I have to change the driver's jumpers?  I didn't touch the jumpers, and
>the new driver booted up instead of the original one.

SCSI devices must have a unique target ID.  For bare drives, this
is usually done by changing the address jumpers.  Older StorageWorks
drives get their ID from the physical shelf slot they're placed in.
The StorageWorks stackbox enclosures provide dip switches for setting
the address of the slot.  If you duplicate an ID already on a bus
any number of things can happen.

So, if the new drive's jumper settings for the SCSI target ID would
duplicate the ID of something already on the bus, yes, you need to
change them.

>
>3.  After I successfully connect a storage system on the scsi adapter,
>what syntax do I have to use to mount the storage system to the hard
>drive?

Generally the steps I use are:

1.  Use scu(8) to scan the particular bus to find the new
    devices:

	# scu scan edt bus #
	# scu show edt bus #

2.  For each new device found by the scan, create the appropriate
    special device file (I include literal examples):

	# cd /dev
	# ./MAKEDEV rz13 tz45 rzc16

    See the SCSI, RAID, rz and tz manual pages for how to derive
    the names.

3.  For disk devices, label them, create file systems on them, add
    them LSM, add them to an existing AdvFS domain, etc.  The
    particulars depend entirely what you want to do with the space.

4.  Mount the new file system.

