In article <3633C197.254@u.washington.edu>, Somckit Khemmanivanh <somckit@u.washington.edu> writes:
>
>Yes, Digital Unix has an LVM like product called LSM, but you will need
>to pay extra for in on DU (just like you need to pay extra for similar
>software on Solaris).  Whereas the LVM is a core part of AIX.

LSM comes with the base system on Digital UNIX.  Some features do
require a license, but some don't.  LSM replaces a version of LVM
(the one IBM contributed to the Open Software Foundation), that
is still in the system.  The base system LSM provides all the
features that LVM (as we shipped it) offers.  Volume concatenation
and slicing up such chucks of space is part of the base system.

Striping and mirror are part of the licensed features.  The GUI
is also part of the licensed features.  I don't know where growing
and shrinking volumes fits in, but there's nothing in the system
to take advantage of it anyway.

>That's a plus for me.
>
>Somckit
