In article <36225BE9.501DF48C@texas.net>, 
    Jean-Claude Magras <magrasjc@texas.net> writes:

> Greetings,
> 
> Can anyone tell me the types of memory an Alphastation 200/233
> uses? What are good and reliable sources for this memory? FAQ?
> 

   72pin, 60 or 70nsec SIMMs with true parity. I don't believe
EDO SIMMs will work. The SIMMs have to be added in pairs. There is
room for 3 pairs of SIMMs on the motherboard ( max 384MB memory using
64MB SIMMs ).

   The SIMMS should have tin plated edge connectors ( like the
sockets on the motherboard.

   I've heard McGlen Micro (www.mcglen.com) is a good place
to buy memory - I haven't actually tried them though. Their prices look
pretty reasonable - and they appear to know what an Alphastation is.

   ps. The 64MB parity SIMMs onsale.com has been selling recently have
GOLD plating on the edge connector ( at least the ones they sold
me did ). I've gotten mixed information on whether or not these should
be used in an Alphastation. I believe they'll work, but in the long
term you might be damaging the motherboard by using them.

