On Wed, 21 Oct 1998, Niel Markwick wrote:

> Yingwei Peng wrote by email:
> > 
> > Thank you for telling me this web site. I just read it briefly
> > and it seemed that I have a lot of work to do. For example, if my
> > understanding is correct, var2 = var1 + i is no longer valid, where
> > var1 and var2 are two pinters of double and i is an integer, because
> > of 64bit pointers in DEC!? I will check this document carefully
> > BTW there was no warning message when I compiled my codes.
> > 
> 
> It is not 'invalid' as such, it just may cause problems if  'i' and var2
> are not defined portably (ie involving sizeof(double))
> 
> However, there are more clean and portable ways of doing this... eg if
> 	var1 is pointer to an array of double's
> 	var2 wants to be pointer to 'n'th double in the array
> then
> 	double *var2 = &var1[n];
> will work on any platform with no problems (and is a bit more readable
> IMHO!)

In C, both 
  var1[n]  
and  
  *(var1+n)  
have the same effect; when adding an integer to a pointer, the size of what 
this pointer points to will be taken into account automatically. What's more, 
you can even write 
  n[var1] 
this, too, is equivalent to *(var1+n).
In other words, if both var1 and var2 are arrays of double, you may write
  7[var2] = 4[var1]
to copy the value of the 4th element of var1 into the 7th element of var2.
Yes, I know this is ugly and I didn't believe it when I saw it the first
time, but nothing in the language standard forbids it, and at least some 
compilers will actually accept this (I tested this with gcc 2.8.1).

Of course, &var1[n] may well be the most readable way to code.

> Also, remember that if var1 is a pointer returned from malloc, where the
> size has been non-portably defined (eg "16" instead of 
> "4*sizeof(double)" for a 4 - double array), then there may not be enough
> valid memory for the required storage, so var2 will point to invalid
> memory.

True.

> Finally, If you are going to stick with the original code, 'i' should
> not be an integer, but size_t (a portable scalar type used for pointer
> arithmetic, defined in <stddef.h>), and the calculation of the value of
> 'i' should somewhere involve 'sizeof(double)' to get the offset right!

Using size_t instead of int is a good idea, but you will certainly not 
have to involve sizeof(double) to get the correct offset for a pointer
into an array of double!

> As a humorous aside, in the past, I have been chastised for using
> sizeof(...) in my code - the pointy haired boss in question told me that
> calling sizeof so may times was a performance issue, and he wanted me to
> do something like:
> 	#define SIZEOF_POINTER 8
> in to avoid the extra function calls!
> 
> It took a LOT of persuasion to convince him that it wasn't really a
> function call, and the compiler simply replaced sizeof(blah) with a
> scalar during compilation!

Could have been worse. A really pointy haired boss would have asked you
to do it in a more portable way (avoiding the hard-coded constant 8):

	int sizeof_pointer;

	main() {
		sizeof_pointer = sizeof(int *);
			.
			.
			.
	}

and use the global variable whenever you need the size of a pointer ;-)


