This is the xmodmap file that I use.  It is not exactly an LK201, but I like it.

!-------------------------------------------------------------------------
! .dxkeycaps.pcxal
!
! This is an `xmodmap' input file for the PCXAL (American) keyboard.
! This file was automatically generated on Thu Jul  6 16:22:08 1995
! by harris with /usr/bin/X11/dxkeycaps.  It attempts to make the PCXAL
! keyboard more like the LK201 keyboard (control key is to the left of the 'A'
! key where it should be!)
!
! Use the following command to load these settins:
!
!       /usr/bin/X11/xmodmap $HOME/.dxkeycaps.lk401
!
! Use the /usr/bin/X11/dxkeycaps to examine the settings after executing
! xmodmap.
!
! Key remapping:
!
!    Caps Lock swapped with Control so that the more frequently used Control
!          key is in a more comfortable position.
!    F8 generates ^Z (an OpenVMS'ism, except that on an LK201 it would
!          actually be F10, but on the PCXAL keyboard the F8 position is
!          physically closer to where the LK201 F10 key was, and it is
!          touch typing finger memory I'm trying to duplicate)
!    F9 generates <ESC> (on an LK201 it would actually be F11, but on the
!          PCXAL keyboard the F9 position is physically closer to where the
!          LK201 F11 key was, and it is touch typing finger memory I'm
!          trying to duplicate)
!    \ key (backslash) generates Carriage Return to give me an oversized
!          Return key.  I found that I was frequently hitting backslasH when
!          I wanted return, so I just moved backslash to the Right Control
!          key and turned the backslash key into a Return key.
!    Right Control key generates \ (backslash) and <Shift> | (vertical bar)
!    The center keypad is mapped to match the LK201
!          Find   Insert  Cut
!          Select Up      Down
!    The numeric keypad is mapped to generate PF1-PF4 along the top row (PF4
!          being the OpenVMS "DO" key.
!    The large double long + (plus) key on the numeric keypad has been
!    remapped to be , (comma) and <Shift> - (minus).  This was a compromise
!          since the LK201 has 2 separate keys for comma and minus.
!

