In <6v2mt1$74e$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com> lloydy1090@my-dejanews.com writes:

>I want to be able to set a variable to the current date minus 7. I'm using
>date +"%d-%b-%y"to return the current date and I want to be able to return
>the same format for 1 week ago.

I don't have *exactly* what you're looking for, but I have two
suggestions that come fairly close.

First of all, there's a program called "calcdate" in Volume 3, Issue 22
of comp.sources.misc.  AltaVista reports a copy at

       http://ftp.doc.ic.ac.uk/packages/unix-c/calendars/calcdate.c.gz

I also have a program I wrote myself to do a subset of what calcdate
does.  In case anyone might find it useful, I'll append the C source to
this message.  To use it to return the date of one week ago today, type

       % dateoffset -7

When executed on October 8th, 1998, the result will be

       1998/10/01

It wouldn't take much work to modify this to produce the same format as
`date +"%d-%b-%y"`.

     - Steven


8<----------------------------   cut here   -------------------------->8
/*
 *
 *  Steven Winikoff  (smw@alcor.concordia.ca)
 *  1997/04/25
 *
 *  dateoffset -- compute today's date plus an offset into the future
 *
 *
 *  usage:  dateoffset  [number_of_days]
 *
 *          if a number of days is specified, we'll print the date
 *          corresponding to that many days into the future from now
 *          (or into the past if the number is negative);  if no number
 *          is supplied, we'll default to printing tomorrow's date;
 *          the output format is "yyyy/mm/dd", eg "1997/04/25"
 *
 *  DISCLAIMER:  This program is made freely available, but with
 *               NO WARRANTY of any kind, express or implied.  Use
 *               at your own risk.  I believe that it works (and yes,
 *               I use it regularly myself :-), but if it doesn't work
 *               for you, and in failing causes you to lose your job,
 *               your spouse, your children and your entire life
 *               savings, neither I nor my employer will be accept
 *               any responsibility (although I will sympathize :-).
 */

/*--------------------------------------------------------------------*/

/**  includes:  **/

#include <stdio.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <time.h>

/*--------------------------------------------------------------------*/

/**  defines:  **/

#define USAGE           "usage:  dateoffset [number_of_days]\n"

#define DEFAULT_OFFSET  1   /* how many days, if not user-specified? */

#define SECONDS_PER_DAY (60 * 60 * 24)

#define BASE_YEAR       1900  /* see localtime(3) */

/*--------------------------------------------------------------------*/

/***   entry here:   ***/

main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
   int       number_of_days;
   struct tm *now;
   time_t    time_in_seconds;


   /**  check calling syntax:  **/

   if      (argc == 2) number_of_days = atoi(argv[1]);
   else if (argc == 1) number_of_days = DEFAULT_OFFSET;
   else
   {
      (void) fprintf(stderr, USAGE);
      exit(1);
   }


   /**  get the current date:  **/

   (void) time(&time_in_seconds);


   /**  add the appropriate offset:  **/

   time_in_seconds += (number_of_days * SECONDS_PER_DAY);


   /**  convert to local time and print the appropriate fields:  **/

   now = localtime(&time_in_seconds);

   (void)printf("%4.4d/%2.2d/%2.2d\n",
               (BASE_YEAR + now->tm_year), now->tm_mon+1, now->tm_mday);


   /**  make lint happy:  **/

   return(0);

} /* end of main() */
