In article <6vju3c$emk$1@newsflash.concordia.ca>, smw@trm.concordia.ca (
Steven Winikoff ) wrote:

>In <6v2mt1$74e$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com> lloydy1090@my-dejanews.com writes:
>
>>I want to be able to set a variable to the current date minus 7. I'm using
>>date +"%d-%b-%y"to return the current date and I want to be able to return
>>the same format for 1 week ago.
>
>I don't have *exactly* what you're looking for, but I have two
>suggestions that come fairly close.
>
>First of all, there's a program called "calcdate" in Volume 3, Issue 22
>of comp.sources.misc.  AltaVista reports a copy at
>
>       http://ftp.doc.ic.ac.uk/packages/unix-c/calendars/calcdate.c.gz


  GNU _date_ can do this via its "-d" flag, used to tell _date_ what time
to pretend it is.  The syntax accepted by "-d" is quite flexible.

  To have GNU _date_ return what you require, you could incant:

      $ date -d "-7days" +%d-%b-%y


  GNU _date_ is part of the GNU Shell Utilities package, which can be found at:

     ftp://ftp.gnu.org/pub/gnu/sh-utils-1.16.tar.gz

-- 
Chris Weber <weber@NOSPAM.bucknell.edu>
