Last week there was a thread here about using an external FNSE SCSI device
with a second (since there is no external connector for the first one, you must
have a second SCSI controller to use external SCSI devices on the Personal
Workstation au) Qlogic 1040 UW.  A couple of people indicated that you needed
a special (very expensive) cable to hook a narrow SCSI device to the
controller.

I was still waiting for my Qlogic SCSI controller to show up at that time, but
I have it now.  As far as I can tell, one does *not* need any kind of special
cable (other than one that has 68 pins on one end and the appropriate connector
on the other end for your device) to use the Qlogic 1040 UW with a narrow
external device.  Perhaps I was misunderstanding what others were saying, or
perhaps they were talking about some other SCSI controller.  One doesn't need
a special cable with the Qlogic card though, which as far as I know is the
only one Digital sells for the PWS.  DEC identifies this card as KZPBA-CA.  It
shows up at boot time as "QLOGIC ISP1040B/V2":

Oct 10 01:09:27 dogbert vmunix: isp1 at pci1000 slot 10
Oct 10 01:09:28 dogbert vmunix: isp1: QLOGIC ISP1040B/V2
Oct 10 01:09:28 dogbert vmunix: isp1: Firmware revision 5.54 (loaded by console)
Oct 10 01:09:28 dogbert vmunix: isp1: Fast RAM timing enabled.
Oct 10 01:09:28 dogbert vmunix: scsi3 at isp1 slot 0
Oct 10 01:09:28 dogbert vmunix: rz27 at scsi3 target 3 lun 0 (LID=3) (SEAGATE ST15230N         0638)


I've been using my PWS with an external narrow hard drive (Seagate ST15230N)
using a plain old Joe Random SCSI cable, with zero problems.

I *did* do some card configuration after I hooked up the external narrow drive.
No idea if this was necessary or not, but here's what I did:

1) at the SRM prompt, do

	ecu

2) when you see the messages about SCSI bios configuration and Fast!UTIL, press
alt-q, to get into the Fast!UTIL.

3) For my PWS, I saw two "PCI Ultra-W" controllers, one at I/O addr 1000 (which
is probably always at that address, and is the on-board controller), the second
one at I/O addr 1400.  I chose the one at 1400, since that's the card for the
external connector.

4) From there, it should be enough to do "Configuration Settings->Autoconfigure
SCSI Devices".  The card will look at SCSI addresses 0-7 (wide addresses 8-15
are not supported by Digital Unix, AFAIK) and will automatically set "Negotiate
Wide" to "No" for any narrow devices.  In my case, it found the ST15230N at
id 3 and realized it was narrow and set the "Negotiate Wide" to "No".

5) Exit and save settings, and then power cycle the workstation (or follow
the maze to get back to the SRM console, whichever you prefer).


