No, all you need is the vanilla 4.0D. Depending on your network setup,
you may however have to add static routes pointing to the devices on 
the extra network (and possibly routes pointing to your server on these
devices, too). We are in a similar situation, actually: all our
class C networks are out of addresses, so that I had to hang more 
than a dozen of new machines off a private 192.168.?.? network, and
add an alias on the DHCP server's ethernet to recongnize boot requests
from these boxes.

Now, if someone could explain me why on earth DU's rpc.statd and rpc.lockd
have to bind to the primary interface address instead of the generic
0.0.0.0, thus breaking NFS locking on file systems mounted via the alias
IP adress ... (Yes, I know how to work around it. However, it should not
be necessary to actually trace system calls and disassemble these beasts
to figure out how to do it.)

Cheers,

/Serge.P



