Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

Hello.  I am having problems running large executables on my Microway
alpha machine running Digital UNIX 4.0D.  My machine has 512 MB of RAM
and a 2 GB swap partition.

Here is the problem:  When executables get around or above 400MB or so,
the machine just locks up, sometimes taking around an hour to respond to
login requests, mouse movements, keyboard input, etc.  The problem seems
to be worse when the executables become that large quickly (within a few
minutes).  When the executables grow slowly, it seems the system is more
efficient in paging and swapping since the system is constanly be
prodded to respond to user input as well as the demands of the
executable as it grows.  When the executable grows quickly, however, it
grabs all the memory resources and then the system won't reallocate them
when needed.

I talked to the DEC people and they just referred me to the ``System
Tuning and Performance Guide.''  So I looked through that and made two
changes:
	1. lowered ubc-maxpercent to 50 (/etc/sysconfigtab)
	2. switched swapping to deferred (lazy) swap mode
This seemed to improve the behavior for slow growing executables, but
didn't do much for the fast growing ones.

If anyone has any suggestions or has run into this problem themself,
please respond.  Thank you.

DAVID.
