 The entries in /etc/disktab are templates  - you can have lots of
disks of the same type on a system, but only need one disktab
entry per disk type. At some version of DIGITAL UNIX, the
disktab file became superfluous anyway (I think it was 3.2x, but
can't remember exactly when).  You can then do:

disklabel -rw rz0  pqr

(where pqr is an arbitrary string, hopefully not a disk type in
/etc/disktab). and the disk will be interrogated for appropriate
parameters and a label will be setup, which you can then edit
as you wish. using the -e option with disklabel. As long as
you then don't use the -w option again, the partitions on the
disks can bear no relation to the entries in /etc/disktab.
Regards,
John Hill

