	We've recently obtained a 9G drive for one of our Alpha servers
and will soon be obtaining an 18G internal drive for another Alpha.
Because the space on both these disks will be spread across several
filesystems on their respective machines, I was contemplating splitting
each of these disks up into equal, smaller units (for the 9G disk, units
of approx. 1G each; for the 18G disk, units of approx. 2G each), which
could then be added to the file domains "behind" various file systems as
needed.  My thinking was that this approach would allow me to easily
extend current filesystems in a generic way, without having to do a great
deal of situation-dependent configuration.
	I recognize that this would not afford an easy way to _reduce_ the
size of the filesystems involved, but that is not a major concern.
	My question is whether anyone can envision any problems with such
an approach.  Is there perhaps a better way to take advantage of the LVM
system in a generic way?


								- Bret -




