> In detail: Timefinder is a functionality of the storagebackend. It
> creates a "Snapshot" of the disk - this snapshot can be made available
> to another System and backed up there without interrupting your
> production system. The only problem you have, that you need a short
> moment when you split the disk, to ensure that the data is valid and al=
l
> cache info of the cpu-frontend is written to disk. This is easy on DB
> Files (you set the database to backup mode, split the disk and set it u=
p
> to online mode again) - but what happens with logbased Filesystems (lik=
e
> AdvFS)? Is the data readable again? What about a Multi-Volume
> Filesystem? Timefinder can split multiple disks simultanously, but what=

> about the integrity of the splitted filesystem(s)?

Why bother with that????

AdvFS has a facility called CLONES, whish does exactly that. A clone of a=
 file
set is a read only snapshot of that file set in the moment of creation. A=
nd it
is on the system level, so all system activities are taken care of.

The only thing you must have is an ADVFS-UTILITIES licensse.

Nix.





