Nikola Milutinovic wrote:
> 
 
> Why bother with that????
> 
> AdvFS has a facility called CLONES, whish does exactly that. A clone of a file
> set is a read only snapshot of that file set in the moment of creation. And it
> is on the system level, so all system activities are taken care of.
> 
> The only thing you must have is an ADVFS-UTILITIES licensse.


Not quite the only thing. clones are great but you should be away of
how they work. 

	when you create a clone of a fileset advfs starts
keeping before images of anything that gets updated in the original
fileset , just like a real database only chunchy :) As such the clone
starts using space within the domain that backs the fileset, so you
need enouch free space in the domain to hold updates for as long
as the clones exists, ie right up to rmfset

..stop db on fileset
/usr/sbin/clonefset domain fileset clonename
mount domain#clonename /someplace
..backup somplace
umount /someplace
rmfset domain clonename

So if this is a busy fileset then you may need to add some floating disk
space to the domain or even make it larger than needed normally just so
you can clone it at backup time.

I, and I guess other have used this quite successfully to backup
various databases and allow the database maximum uptime.

	Cheers
		Mark :)



