From: alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com (Alan Rollow - Dr. File System's Home for Wayward Inodes.)
Subject: Re: Dirty File System
Date: 1998/10/16
Message-ID: <707vag$1lg$1@nntpd2.cxo.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 401867161
Distribution: world
References: <702trh$r1j$1@news-2.news.gte.net>
Organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
Reply-To: alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com (Alan Rollow - Dr. File System's Home for Wayward Inodes.)
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


When a UFS is mounted, a field in the file system superblock is
modified to indicate the file system is mounted; potentially
dirty.  When it is unmounted the field is changed to indicated
this and thus probably clean.  The assumption being that the
file system code keeps the file system in a consistent state
while a file system is mounted.  If the system crashes or some-
thing else happens to the file system, the "dirty" state won't
be cleared and the file system may be inconsistent.

Whatever the cause, that the state your file system is in.  The
mount code hasn't found the value indicating that the file system
was unmounted cleanly.  Fix it and whatever other problems there
may be with the file system, use fsck(8).
--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com




