Massimo Gais (mgais@na.astro.it) wrote:
: Nikola Milutinovic wrote:
: > 
: > Massimo Gais wrote:
: > >
: > > Hello,
: > >
: > > I'd like to know what means the message
: > > "affinity set to primary CPU"
: > > that DU shows at booting.
: > 
: > DEC Alpha servers can be a multi-processor machines. When UNIX boot it starts
: > only one processor. The message simply says which processor it is booting on.
: > 
: > Later "in the day", UNIX will start all other processors. Well, it'll be done
: > before multi-user run levels. In fact, I think it'll be done even before single
: > user mode.
: > 
: > I believe I heard that you need a special license, SMP, for activating
: > multi-processor UNIX.
: > 
: > Nix.

: Ok, thank you for your explaination, but...
: why they talk about "affinity"? It is not enough to say
: "Booting CPU #1" ?
: I mean: do I miss some deeper philosophical consideration?

Some activities can only be performed on a single cpu.  For example, updating
the system time should only be done on a single cpu, typically the primary
cpu.  The assigning of an activity to a cpu is referred to as setting the
activity's "affinity".

The primary cpu is typically the one that started the boot.

        Stu



